I had it happen yesterday, so it's definitely possible: in that game,
I lucked out with teleport control on my first tengu, and that
character doesn't yet have teleportitis.  (I've avoided getting close
to any live nymphs, and my pets keep eating all the corpses.)

I think one of the first times I ate a tengu, I got teleport control.
So, obviously, in the next three or four games I played, I ate tengus
whenever I could find them.  Wouldn't you know it, I got teleport
control every time!  I started to think it was a guaranteed thing,
like telepathy from a floating eye.

Then, of course, a very promising early character ate a tengu in the
mines and caught teleportitis.  While arranging gear in a cache before
descending (to find more tengus, I hoped), he was teleported away from
his cache and pets, fell down a hole, was surrounded while
overburdened, dropped most of his gear, was teleported away from the
battle and his stuff, stepped on a level teleport trap into *another*
unexplored mine level, and was killed by some esoteric creature I'd
never before seen (probably coming from a polymorph trap).

It's just one of those things where personal experience convinces you
there's an unwritten rule at play that isn't really there.  For the
longest time, I'd unwield my weapon when killing something whose
corpse was desirable.  I was convinced that a "gentler kill" was much
more likely to leave a corpse.  I guess it seemed to make sense, and
when I tried it, my early experience seemed to bear it out.

I now know that's bogus, but I shudder to think how many other things
I do that are wrong or pointless because I've been convinced by the
RNG that they're necessary or helpful.

